  "output": "Note: this looks like a repeat of a prior analysis (session S170, Jul 9). Rather than re-run generic passes, here's a focused, actionable set based on the actual code shown.\n\n## Caching\n\n**`src/utils/cache.ts` — no TTL or size bound.** `cacheGet`/`cacheSet` write to localStorage indefinitely; `pruneOldCache` only drops old-version keys, not stale/oversized ones. If large payloads (transcripts, campaign lists) get cached, this risks `QuotaExceededError` (silently swallowed by the try/catch, so failures go unnoticed).\n\n```ts\nexport function cacheSet<T>(key: string, value: T, ttlMs?: number) {\n  try {\n    const payload = { value, expiresAt: ttlMs ? Date.now() + ttlMs : null };\n    window.localStorage.setItem(`${CACHE_PREFIX}${key}`, JSON.stringify(payload));\n  } catch { /* best-effort */ }\n}\n```\n\n## Redundant computations\n\n**`src/utils/transcript.ts:buildConversationItems`** runs `events.filter()`, `.map()` twice, and a final `.sort()` on every call — fine as a pure function, but if called per-render in a view without memoizing on `(transcript, events)` identity, it recomputes on every keystroke/tick elsewhere in the tree. Wrap the call site in `useMemo`:\n\n```ts\nconst items = useMemo(() => buildConversationItems(transcript, events), [transcript, events]);\n```\n\n**`src/utils/formatting.ts:parseApiDate`** — regex test + `Date` construction runs repeatedly for the same transcript rows across `formatDate`/`formatTime`/`timeAgo` calls on the same value. Not costly individually, but in a list view rendering hundreds of transcript rows, this adds up. Low priority.\n\n## Memory leaks\n\nNone evident in the shown files — `useKeyboardShortcuts` and `useToasts` clean up correctly (event listener removed, refs avoid stale closures). `useCountUp`'s `requestAnimationFrame` loop isn't cancelled on unmount if `target` changes mid-animation before the effect cleanup — minor: add `cancelAnimationFrame` via a ref if this hook is used with frequently-changing `target` values.\n\n## N+1 / re-renders / App.tsx\n\nI don't have `App.tsx`, view components, or backend query code in this truncated context — the memory log (obs 1389/1393/1395) already flagged `TranscriptsView` re-renders, sequential data-loading effects in `App.tsx`, and un-memoized campaign strategy objects from yesterday's pass. Those weren't in what was pasted here, so I can't verify line numbers without reading the actual files.\n\nWant me to open `App.tsx` and `TranscriptsView` directly to confirm those still apply and get exact line numbers, or is this analysis sufficient?\n",
